## Rules (real-robot rules)
- **Time does not stop.** The episode starts paused, on a fixed starting state, the robot standing still. Its clock starts at your first `submit` (or `get_obs(seconds=s)`) and from then on the robot runs at 50 Hz in real time, one control step every 0.02 s of wall-clock time, whether or not you send anything, until the episode ends. Between your commands it executes what you planned, then holds your last command with zero walking and turning velocity (it stands still where it is); nothing pauses for you, not your thinking, not your tool calls.
- **You are far too slow to steer it turn by turn.** One turn of yours takes seconds. Drive it with a program: a controller of your own (Python in your container, with numpy and the client) that runs a loop, reads the robot and sends commands, started in the background (`setsid nohup python -u /app/output/gen.py > /app/output/run.log 2>&1 < /dev/null &`: `setsid` matters, a plain `nohup ... &` is killed when your shell command returns) so that your session is free while it runs.
- **One episode, no reset.** `spec()["budget"]["reset"]` is authoritative: it is 0 here, so the one episode is the run.
- **No privileged state, no score.** You see what the robot's three cameras see (RGB, @@IMAGE_HW@@×@@IMAGE_HW@@: `head`, `left_wrist`, `right_wrist`) and its own proprioception (below). Not where any object is now (only the start sentence above), not the success check: the service judges the episode and only tells you that it ended and why (`meta["done"]`, `meta["reason"]`). `spec()` gives the robot's facts: the action slots and their bounds, each camera's field of view and its pose at the start.
- There is nothing to hand in: the service records every control step it ran, replays the episode in a fresh simulator and grades the replay. Keep the controller at `/app/output/gen.py` for the record: save it early and keep it current.
- Wall-clock budget: 1 h. It ends without warning, and ending your session earlier ends the run the same way (and stops your background programs), so do not stop early.

## Success
@@SUCCESS_PAR@@

The episode ends when the success has held for 1 s (`reason = "success"`)@@ENDS_FAIL@@, when the pelvis gets more than
10 m from the scene's origin, on a simulator error, after **@@HORIZON_STEPS@@ control steps** (@@HORIZON_S@@ s after its
clock started), or when your time is up. A fresh replay of the commands the robot actually executed must succeed
as well and end in the same state.

## Time and streaming
- `submit` answers at once, with the robot as it is now: the rows you sent have not run yet. They become the plan from control step `at` (an episode step number; by default the next step), replacing whatever was planned from `at` on. When the plan runs out, the robot stands still on your last command; a gap you leave with a later `at` is filled with the plan's last row instead, its walking and turning velocity included. `res.meta["control_steps"]` is the number of steps run so far, `res.meta["proprio"]["now"]` the robot's proprioception now, `res.meta["plan_end_step"]` where the plan now ends.
- Closed loop: every 0.1-0.2 s send the next 0.1-0.2 s of rows (`r.submit("whole_body", A, dt=0.02, cameras=[])`), computed from the proprioception the previous call returned, so the plan never runs out between your calls (when it does, the robot stands still). `cameras=[]` skips the images (the fastest call).
- Open loop: send up to 10 s of rows at once (`dt` a multiple of 0.02) and the next chunk before the plan runs out, with `at` = the step where it should take over; `at` may be up to `spec()["timing"]["max_ahead_steps"]` steps ahead.
- A `linear` chunk starts its interpolation from the command in force just before `at`: the row planned for that step, or, once the plan has run out, the standing row the robot holds.
- A row whose step has already run when the request arrives is dropped (`res.meta["late_steps"]` counts them). `get_obs()` reads the robot now; `get_obs(seconds=s)` (at most 30) waits while s seconds of robot time run and returns what the cameras saw. `spec()["timing"]` states all this; `r.status()` shows `running` and `lag_s`.

## Client (`/opt/tools/rcb_client.py`, protocol `eai-standard/2.3`, standard-realtime mode)
Its docstring is the full reference (`python -c "import rcb_client; help(rcb_client)"`); here the action type is
`"whole_body"` (not the docstring's example name):

| call | endpoint | what it does |
|---|---|---|
| `r = Robot()` | | connects (`http://sim:8000`, then `http://localhost:8000`) and waits until the scene is ready |
| `r.spec()` | `GET /spec` | the robot's facts: `action_types["whole_body"]` (layout, lower, upper, units), `control_dt` (0.02 s), `max_control_steps` (@@HORIZON_STEPS@@), `timing`, the cameras, `proprio_layout`, `budget` |
| `r.status()` | `GET /status` | `calls`, `rows`, `control_steps`, `done`, `reason`, `elapsed_s`, `running`, `planned_steps`, `lag_s` |
| `r.submit("whole_body", A, dt=0.02, interpolate="linear", at=None, cameras=None)` | `POST /submit` | plans `A`, rows of @@DIM@@ commands `dt` seconds apart (a chunk covers at most 10 s), from step `at`; answers at once; the first one starts the clock |
| `r.get_obs(cameras=None, seconds=0, video="frames")` | `POST /observe` | the robot now, or what the cameras saw over the next `seconds` |

Measured here on an otherwise idle host: the simulator spends about 14 ms of each 20 ms control step, so it keeps up
with the clock (largest lag seen 0.07 s); `get_obs()` without seconds answers in about 10 ms. Values outside the bounds are clipped and reported in `meta["clipped"]`, never refused; a malformed request raises
`ValueError` and changes nothing.

## Action rows (@@DIM@@ floats, physical units)
@@LAYOUT_TABLE@@

The last column is the row that stands still at the start. @@CONTROLLER@@

`spec()["joints"]` gives each arm, waist and finger joint's axis, a point on it and its range (the pelvis frame with
every joint at 0: `spec()["joint_frame"]`; the sign convention: `spec()["joint_sign"]`), `spec()["grippers"]` the point between each gripper's finger
pads on its wrist link, and `spec()["cameras"][camera]["mount"]` each camera's pose on its link. Keep your own copy of the last row you sent: a row rebuilt from the measured joint angles sits a little below
the targets the arms were holding (the servos track with a small error), so holding with it lets the arms sag. The
client's docstring explains the protocol with another benchmark's examples (`joint_position`, bounds [-1, 1], `imu`,
`links`, `depth`): for this task `spec()` is authoritative.

